---
import BaseLayout from '../../layout/BaseLayout.astro'
import { destinations } from '../../layout/navigation'

/**
 * /privacy/: what data the website, the desktop app, a connected AI tool and
 * support each involve, kept apart (spec §9, ticket 1aefd5aa). Every statement
 * comes from the host and support decisions (D-044, D-045) or from the app's
 * code; the sources are listed on the ticket. Change the date when the text
 * changes.
 */
const updated = '2026-10-21'
const updatedLabel = new Date(`${updated}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
})
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What data the Knightbook website, the desktop app, a connected AI tool and support each involve."
>
  <article class="kb-page privacy">
    <header>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="updated">
        Last updated <time datetime={updated}>{updatedLabel}</time>
      </p>
      <p>
        Knightbook involves four separate things: this website, the desktop app, the AI tool you
        connect to it, and the places you can ask for help. Each handles data differently, so each
        has its own section.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="website">
      <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
      <p>
        The website is hosted on GitHub Pages, and installers are downloaded from GitHub Releases.
        GitHub logs the IP address of every visitor to the website and of every download, for
        security purposes. What GitHub keeps and for how long is set by
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">
          GitHub's privacy statement
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <p>
        The website itself runs no analytics or tracking script, sets no cookies, and loads nothing
        from other sites: its pages are plain HTML, CSS and images served by GitHub Pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="app">
      <h2 id="app">The desktop app</h2>
      <p>
        Your projects stay in the folders you choose. Knightbook keeps its tickets, plans and review
        records in a <code>.projectspace</code> folder inside each project, beside your own files.
        On your computer it also keeps its settings, the list of recent projects, the window's size
        and position, and which projects you allowed AI access to.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app's own code makes no network requests: it sends nothing about you or your projects
        anywhere, has no account, and checks for no updates. Images in documents are shown only from
        inside the project, never from the internet. A web link in a document opens in your browser
        only when you click it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app is built on Electron, which includes a spell checker. According to Electron's
        documentation, on Windows and Linux it downloads spell-check dictionaries from Google's
        servers by default. We have not measured whether Knightbook does this.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="ai">
      <h2 id="ai">The AI tool you connect</h2>
      <p>
        Knightbook does not include an AI assistant. When you connect an AI tool, it reads and
        changes the projects you allowed through Knightbook's connection, which runs on your
        computer. What that tool, and the AI provider behind it, then send, keep or use is up to
        them and their terms, not Knightbook. Read their privacy terms before allowing a project
        with private content.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="support">
      <h2 id="support">Getting help</h2>
      <p>
        Support is through
        <a href={destinations.support.href}>GitHub Issues</a>. Everything in an issue, including
        your GitHub username, is public and handled by GitHub under its privacy statement. Leave out
        private project content, credentials and unredacted logs.
      </p>
      <p>
        A security problem is reported privately through GitHub instead. Only you and the maintainer
        can read a private report, and it is made public only once a fix is out, if at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contact">
      <h2 id="contact">Questions about this page</h2>
      <p>
        Knightbook is maintained by Dennis Ninic. Ask about this page in a
        <a href={destinations.support.href}>GitHub issue</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy {
    max-width: 72ch;
    padding-block: var(--kb-space-8) 0;
  }

  .updated {
    color: var(--kb-muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  section {
    margin-top: var(--kb-space-6);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.375rem;
  }
</style>
